From: substrate
Subject: kdrive/evdev: filter BTN_* codes on the keyboard fd

Substrate's input layer multiplexes every device through a single
/dev/input/event0 stream, so the kdrive evdev keyboard fd sees not
only KEY_* presses but also BTN_LEFT / BTN_RIGHT / BTN_MIDDLE
(0x110..0x112) when the mouse moves.

EvdevKbdRead unconditionally hands every EV_KEY event to
KdEnqueueKeyboardEvent(ki, code, ...), whose `scan_code` parameter is
`unsigned char`.  BTN_LEFT (0x110) truncates to 0x10 == KEY_Q;
likewise BTN_RIGHT -> KEY_W, BTN_MIDDLE -> KEY_E.  Result: mouse
buttons type q/w/e into the focused X client.

EvdevPtrBtn already filters with `code >= BTN_MOUSE && code <
BTN_JOYSTICK`, so the pointer fd silently drops real keyboard codes.
Mirror that on the keyboard side: only forward EV_KEY events whose
code is below BTN_MISC (0x100) -- the entire KEY_* range -- so the
mouse-button codes get dropped at the keyboard reader instead of
typed.

--- a/hw/kdrive/linux/evdev.c
+++ b/hw/kdrive/linux/evdev.c
@@ -381,8 +381,16 @@
 
     n /= sizeof(struct input_event);
     for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
-        if (events[i].type == EV_KEY)
+        if (events[i].type == EV_KEY) {
+            /* Substrate multiplexes every device onto a single evdev
+             * stream, so BTN_* (0x100+) events reach this fd along
+             * with real KEY_* (<0x100) presses.  KdEnqueueKeyboardEvent's
+             * scan_code is `unsigned char`, so a BTN_LEFT slips through
+             * as KEY_Q.  Drop everything outside the real-key range. */
+            if (events[i].code >= BTN_MISC)
+                continue;
             KdEnqueueKeyboardEvent(ki, events[i].code, !events[i].value);
+        }
 /* FIXME: must implement other types of events
         else
             ErrorF("Event type (%d) not delivered\n", events[i].type);
